WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.280
A white blood cell racing in blood can stop on a vessel wall in about a second.

2
00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:13.860
Its gripping receptor has no enzyme, so how does it switch on fast and send signals

3
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:14.860
inside?

4
00:00:15.483 --> 00:00:18.323
The receptor that grabs hold is an integrin.

5
00:00:19.523 --> 00:00:24.623
It is built from two different chains, α and β, each crossing the membrane once.

6
00:00:26.403 --> 00:00:30.763
Outside, their large parts form a head that grips the matrix, the protein mesh cells

7
00:00:30.763 --> 00:00:33.063
sit on, or proteins on other cells.

8
00:00:34.543 --> 00:00:38.643
Inside, two short tails face actin, with no kinase and no channel.

9
00:00:39.767 --> 00:00:44.027
A hormone receptor waits quietly until a soluble ligand arrives.

10
00:00:45.447 --> 00:00:48.067
Many integrins are bathed in ligand all the time.

11
00:00:49.387 --> 00:00:53.727
Think of a platelet floating through blood that is already rich in fibrinogen, its

12
00:00:53.727 --> 00:00:57.807
sticky partner. Why does that platelet not clump on the spot?

13
00:01:03.783 --> 00:01:06.503
Because the cell controls the receptor itself.

14
00:01:07.863 --> 00:01:12.043
In platelet and white blood cell integrins, the outer legs fold at a knee.

15
00:01:13.423 --> 00:01:15.863
Bent with the head closed, the grip stays weak.

16
00:01:17.123 --> 00:01:21.963
An inside signal recruits talin and kindlin to the β tail, and the two tails

17
00:01:21.963 --> 00:01:27.643
separate. That change passes through the membrane, so the legs straighten and the

18
00:01:27.643 --> 00:01:28.643
head opens tight.

19
00:01:29.617 --> 00:01:34.617
Nothing new had to be made, so a platelet or white blood cell can grab hold fast.

20
00:01:36.017 --> 00:01:40.837
Because the message starts in the cytoplasm and changes grip outside, this is

21
00:01:40.837 --> 00:01:41.937
inside-out signalling.

22
00:01:43.337 --> 00:01:48.697
Once bound integrins cluster on a solid surface, the message reverses and runs

23
00:01:48.697 --> 00:01:49.697
inward.

24
00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:54.460
Clustered integrins assemble a focal adhesion on their short tails.

25
00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:00.440
Talin links the β tail to actin, and paxillin forms a docking scaffold.

26
00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:05.860
With no enzyme of its own, the cluster borrows focal adhesion kinase.

27
00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:11.680
Folded shut at rest, it opens and phosphorylates one of its own tyrosines.

28
00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.980
That phosphate docks Src, another kinase, and the pair phosphorylates neighbours.

29
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.700
The same adhesion also feels how hard it is being pulled.

30
00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:28.860
The motor protein myosin drags actin inward against the anchored matrix.

31
00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:35.100
Talin's rod hides binding sites for vinculin, a linker that also grips actin.

32
00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.800
Tension stretches the rod and uncovers those buried sites.

33
00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:45.580
Vinculin then clips talin to actin, strengthening the grip without adding any

34
00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:46.880
any phosphate.

35
00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:52.230
Integrin grip switches from inside, and focal adhesions signal and sense force.

36
00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:57.070
Inside cues straighten a bent receptor into a firm hold.

37
00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:03.770
Once bound and pulled, the complex borrows kinases and adds extra links under load.

38
00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:09.190
Whether other adhesion proteins unfold the same way stays open.

39
00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:13.030
Next, actin switches turn grip into movement.
